<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casimir I of Opole</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1178–<a href="/years/1230">1230</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Kazimierz I of Opole</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Casimir I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> (c. 1178–1230) was a Piast duke of Opole and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a> from 1211, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iv-tanglefoot">Mieszko IV Tanglefoot</a>. He encouraged settlement under German law, founding towns and granting them charters, and moved his residence to Opole, where he built a new castle. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-the-fat">Mieszko II the Fat</a> and Władysław succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-opole.jpg" alt="Casimir I of Opole" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous plate · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazimierz_I_opolski_seal_1226.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Casimir was the eldest child and only son of Duke Mieszko Tanglefoot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a> and his wife Ludmilla, probably a Bohemian princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>. His birth was the occasion of an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just">Casimir II the Just</a>, High Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, who wished to break Mieszko's alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iii-the-old">Mieszko III the Old</a>: he gave Mieszko the Lesser Polish districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Oświęcim</a> and Pszczyna and stood godfather to the boy, who was named after him. After the death of his brother Bolesław I the Tall in 1201, Mieszko conquered the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Casimir succeeded his father in 1211. He first joined the coalition of the junior dukes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek the White</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic">Władysław Odonic</a> against Władysław III Spindleshanks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry the Bearded</a>, working closely with the church and with Bishop Wawrzyniec of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. At the Congress of Wolbórz in 1215 he granted the church great privileges and immunity, the origin of the semi-independent district of Ujazd. As Henry grew stronger in the 1220s, Casimir allied with his cousin; his troops joined Henry's failed expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in 1225, and in 1227 he annexed the frontier fortress of Czeladź.</p><p>Following Henry's example, he encouraged German settlers. Towns were founded under German town law at Leśnica in 1217, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' lands of Ujazd in 1222, at Biała and Gościęcin in 1225 and at Olesno in 1226. Settlement helped the duchy's economy, though less than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>. Emigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> also served him, among them Klement of Brzeźnica of the Gryfici family, who paid part of the cost of the city walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Casimir died suddenly on 13 May 1230 and was buried at Czarnowąsy, a monastery he had generously patronised. He married late, probably between 1212 and 1220; the origins of his wife Viola are unknown, though the chronicler Jan Długosz said she came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Their children were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-the-fat">Mieszko II the Fat</a> and Władysław, both dukes of Opole-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>, Wenzeslawa, a nun at Czarnowąsy, and Euphrosyne, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-kuyavia">Casimir I of Kuyavia</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Henry the Bearded became regent for the young sons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casimir I of Opole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kazimierz I</b> and his successors brought in German settlers and granted town charters under German law, founding or refounding places such as Opole and Racibórz.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opole-and-raciborz">Duchy of Opole and Racibórz</a></li></ul></section>
